# PostPlayer — Invariants

The load-bearing truths the reader's player descends from — seek and search promises,
autoplay policy, and the player/scroller contract. Everything player-level in
`PostPlayer.ts` is a consequence of one of these — if you're changing the system, change it
_here_ first and let the rest follow.

The scroller-level invariants (scroll physics, geometry, snap policy, layer discipline)
live in `../virtual-scroller/VirtualScroller.invariants.md`. All of the invariants below
are **chosen** disciplines, and they stand on that doc's **reality** invariants —
especially _Smoothness Is Crossing Regularity_ and _Estimates Are Provisional; Identity
Survives, Pixels Don't_. The dependency only ever points one way: chosen invariants stand
on reality invariants, never the reverse.

---

# Chosen invariants — disciplines; hold them consistently

## The Promise Is An Item, Never A Pixel

Whatever UI promises a destination promises an **item identity**, and the landing owes that
item readability:

- the seek preview promises `ceil(ratio × (len−1))` — the first item fully readable below
  the landed viewport top; height-independent, so refinement can't flip it (see
  _Estimates Are Provisional_ in the scroller doc);
- the landing keeps that item's top at least `SEEK_END_GAP_PX` below the edge (a knife-edge
  gap gets eaten by the creep within a second);
- a search click promises the **match**, landed `container × 0.2` down with the match's
  char-proportional offset inside its paragraph (`innerFraction`).

## The Reader Owns The Scroll

Every programmatic mover yields to reader input, immediately and unconditionally: settle
loops abort on `isScrolling`, the creep defers while lenis has undelivered input, converge
loops disarm the moment the reader takes over. And convergence is **until-quiet**, never a
fixed timer — a timer loses the race against late measurement waves.

## Speed Is Product, Delivery Is Physics

